 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Yes, but only modestly. Queensland's unemployment rate has been drifting up, and it is still a little below the national rate.**\n\n**Queensland, seasonally adjusted, September 2026**\n- The unemployment rate was 4.53%. It was 4.17% in September 2025 and 3.96% in January 2025.\n- About 144,400 people were unemployed. That is up from about 130,600 a year earlier, a rise of roughly 14,000.\n- The rate has risen in the last two months, from 4.24% in July to 4.51% in August and 4.53% in September.\n- The employment-to-population ratio was 63.9%. It was 64.3% in September 2025 and 64.6% in January 2025. So a smaller share of the working-age population has a job.\n\n**Australia**\n- The national unemployment rate was 4.72% in September 2026. It was 4.43% a year earlier and 4.13% in January 2025.\n- Queensland (4.53%) is therefore still below the national rate, and the national rate has risen faster recently.\n\n**Caveats**\n- Queensland's monthly figures are noisy. The rate swung between 3.73% and 4.44% in the first half of 2026, for example. A single month's move shouldn't be over-read.\n- Jobs are still being added. Employment rose by about 65,000 over the 20 months from January 2025 to September 2026, to 3.043 million. The labour force grew faster, by about 87,000, so unemployment rose. Participation is fairly steady at 66.9%.\n\nOverall, jobs are still growing, but not fast enough to absorb the growth in the labour force. Finding work is getting somewhat harder, though the labour market has not deteriorated sharply.",
